  "title": "The founded colony, spaced 4: is climb-over still worth it with no jam to dissolve?",
  "question": "These two are much closer than the 55-ant crowd card. Does either read as a more satisfying colony at work — and is the difference worth changing the ants' default?",
  "context_md": "Same wetland world (seed 2), same 35-ant colony founded by `found_colony`, same 24 frames from noon. One species flag differs.\n\n**Why a second card, when the crowd card is already answered.** That card (`20260823T164147144Z-e1ef72`) used the 55-ant probe scene, which plants ants **2 cells apart** — shoulder to shoulder for a two-cell body, and the exact configuration dead ends 775/829 record as gridlock. It blocks ~61% of its moves. A real colony is founded at `COLONY_ANT_SPACING = 4` and blocks ~24%. So that card asked *does climb-over dissolve a gridlock* — it does, enormously. This one asks whether it still earns its place where there is no gridlock to dissolve.\n\n**Measured, 8 seeds x 24,000 frames on the WP-4 instrument, at colony spacing, medians:** deepest excursion 46 -> 84 cells; excursions past 32 cells 4.5 -> 17.5; past 64 cells 0 -> 4.5; blocked moves 0.311 -> 0.033. **Deliveries flat:** 6.5 vs 6.0, against a per-seed spread of 0-49.\n\nThe counters under each strip come from these two runs, not from another harness's run of a different world.\n\n**One honest cost:** falls roughly double on uneven ground — an animal standing on a nestmate that walks away has further to drop. Deaths are 0 in both arms and the population is the same 74.\n\nPlease ignore any tint drift across the strip; both arms share it.",
